INSERT INTO sectors (sector_id, label, status, framing_text, methodology_notes, last_reviewed_date) VALUES ('quantum', 'Quantum Technologies', 'active', 'Quantum technologies rank among the UK’s most mature frontier sectors, underpinned by more than a decade of sustained public investment and a research base regularly cited among the world’s top three quantum clusters. The 2023 National Quantum Strategy set a ten-year, £2.5bn framework built around five national missions spanning computing, networking, sensing and timing; a further £2bn followed in March 2026, including a landmark procurement programme for domestically built quantum computers. The sector’s policy home changed in July 2026 as part of a wider machinery-of-government reorganisation, and — as the Evolving tab sets out — exactly how quantum-specific leadership continues within the new departmental structure is still becoming clear.', 'Links verified against primary sources (gov.uk, ukri.org, org sites, journal pages) in June–July 2026. Items marked no link verified are confirmed but no URL was retrieved during research. US–UK Technology Prosperity Deal paused Dec 2025; partially restarted Feb 2026. The Evolving tab collects developments that are still genuinely unfolding — governance questions, funding still in flux, or figures that vary by source — as distinct from the settled historical record in the other tabs. In the Breakthroughs section the subtitle field lists key people; the caveat field lists journal citations and accolades (shown highlighted). Import replaces all records for the section(s) present in the uploaded file; other sections are unaffected. Template CSV includes one guide row and one example per type — delete both before importing real data. Valid section values: timeline | institution | initiative | breakthrough | evolving. Initiative rows use type domestic or international.', '2026-07-01') ON CONFLICT (sector_id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO sectors (sector_id, label, status, framing_text, methodology_notes, last_reviewed_date) VALUES ('engbio', 'Engineering Biology', 'active', 'Engineering biology — the application of engineering principles to biological systems — has grown from a research priority first flagged to government in 2012 into one of six official UK frontier technologies. The 2023 National Vision for Engineering Biology set a ten-year, £2bn ambition and described the UK as home to one of the world’s most capable engineering biology ecosystems; public investment has scaled accordingly, most recently through a £380m package spanning research programmes and scale-up infrastructure. The sector also carries a genuine, still-unresolved policy question common to biotechnology internationally: the UK’s streamlined domestic approach to regulating gene-edited crops sits alongside ongoing UK–EU trade negotiations that could shape how far that regulatory path is able to diverge from European norms in the years ahead.', 'Links verified against primary sources (gov.uk, ukri.org, org sites, journal pages, company/institution sites) in August 2026. Items marked no link verified are confirmed but no URL was retrieved during research. US–UK Technology Prosperity Deal (includes a biotechnology/precision medicine research strand) paused 16 Dec 2025; partially restarted Feb 2026 — status of the biotech strand specifically not independently re-verified since. Genetic Technology (Precision Breeding) Act 2023 and its 2025 Regulations apply to England only; Northern Ireland''s GMO regime remains aligned to the EU approach under the Windsor Framework; Scotland and Wales take distinct approaches — see the Institutions tab for devolved-administration detail. The Evolving tab collects developments that are still genuinely unfolding, as distinct from the settled historical record in the other tabs. In the Breakthroughs section the subtitle field lists key people; the caveat field lists journal citations and accolades (shown highlighted). Import replaces all records for the section(s) present in the uploaded file; other sections are unaffected. Template CSV includes one guide row and one example per type — delete both before importing real data. Valid section values: timeline | institution | initiative | breakthrough | evolving. Initiative rows use type domestic or international.', '2026-08-01') ON CONFLICT (sector_id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO sectors (sector_id, label, status, framing_text, methodology_notes, last_reviewed_date) VALUES ('ai', 'Artificial Intelligence', 'active', 'Artificial intelligence occupies a distinct position among the UK’s frontier technologies. It is the sector where Britain has taken the most prominent international convening role — hosting the first global AI Safety Summit in 2023 — and also the one where policy emphasis has visibly shifted since, moving from early safety-focused international coordination toward a stronger domestic emphasis on economic growth, sovereign computing capacity, and national-security-oriented evaluation. The UK is home to Google DeepMind, whose AlphaFold work earned a Nobel Prize in 2024, and to one of Europe’s most active AI investment ecosystems. As of mid-2026, AI has become the first UK technology policy area represented by a dedicated Cabinet minister, even as day-to-day responsibility for delivering AI policy is now divided across separate government departments.', 'Links prioritise primary sources (gov.uk, parliament.uk, company/institution sites) over media coverage in August 2026, per a specific request from the person maintaining this tool — rows still resting on secondary sources are marked accordingly rather than silently upgraded. Items marked no link verified are confirmed but no URL was retrieved during research. The department overseeing non-AI-specific technology policy changed its own working name twice within a week in Jul 2026 (“DBIST” → “BIST”); this tool uses “DBIST”/“the business department” descriptively rather than committing to an acronym — see the Evolving tab. The Evolving tab collects developments that are still genuinely unfolding — most notably the Alan Turing Institute’s governance situation — as distinct from the settled historical record in the other tabs. In the Breakthroughs section the subtitle field lists key people; the caveat field lists clarifying notes. Import replaces all records for the section(s) present in the uploaded file; other sections are unaffected. Template CSV includes one guide row and one example per type — delete both before importing real data. Valid section values: timeline | institution | initiative | breakthrough | evolving. Initiative rows use type domestic or international.', '2026-08-01') ON CONFLICT (sector_id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO sectors (sector_id, label, status, framing_text, methodology_notes, last_reviewed_date) VALUES ('semiconductors', 'Semiconductors', 'active', 'Semiconductors occupies a distinctive position among the UK’s frontier technologies: unlike quantum or engineering biology, Britain has no significant bulk chip-fabrication capacity to rival Taiwan, South Korea or the United States, and the 2023 National Semiconductor Strategy deliberately chose not to compete on that basis. Instead, public funding — a fraction of the scale of the US CHIPS Act or EU Chips Act — concentrates on areas of genuine domestic strength: chip design and IP licensing, the world-leading compound semiconductor cluster in South Wales, and emerging strength in flexible electronics and 2D materials. The sector also carries the UK’s most consequential semiconductor-specific national security episode to date: the 2022 forced divestment of the Newport Wafer Fab from Chinese ownership, resolved by its 2024 sale to Vishay. Since 2025, policy has shifted markedly toward AI hardware, culminating in the June 2026 AI Hardware Plan and the renaming of the Compound Semiconductor Applications Catapult into a broader Semiconductor Catapult — making this the frontier technology most tightly coupled to the UK’s AI ambitions, and, as the Evolving tab sets out, one whose policy ownership within government is still settling after the July 2026 reshuffle.', 'Links prioritise primary sources (gov.uk, parliament.uk, company/institution sites, Companies House, SEC/RNS filings) over media coverage, researched August 2026. Items marked no link verified are confirmed but no URL was retrieved during research. This is the first sector in this tool to use the Regulation tab (Export Controls / National Security Screening / Standards / IP &amp; Security Policy) — reintroduced because semiconductors is more entangled with export-control and investment-screening law than any sector built so far; the Standards sub-tab is left thin deliberately rather than padded with weak material — no dedicated UK semiconductor standards programme was identified during research. The Evolving tab collects developments that are still genuinely unfolding — most notably how semiconductor policy splits between BIST and the Cabinet Office AI Taskforce post-reshuffle — as distinct from the settled historical record in the other tabs. Notable companies (Arm, Pragmatic Semiconductor, Paragraf and others) are referenced within Institutions where directly relevant but are not exhaustively profiled here — see the person’s companion research document for fuller company detail. In the Breakthroughs section the subtitle field lists key people; the caveat field lists clarifying or sourcing notes. Import replaces all records for the section(s) present in the uploaded file; other sections are unaffected. Template CSV includes one guide row and one example per type — delete both before importing real data. Valid section values: timeline | institution | initiative | regulation | breakthrough | evolving. Initiative rows use type domestic or international; regulation rows use type export, nsi, standards or ipsec.', '2026-08-01') ON CONFLICT (sector_id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO sectors (sector_id, label, status, framing_text, methodology_notes, last_reviewed_date) VALUES ('cyber', 'Cybersecurity', 'active', 'Cybersecurity carries the deepest institutional prehistory of any sector in this tool: GCHQ traces its lineage to the 1919 Government Code &amp; Cypher School, and Bletchley Park’s Colossus — built to break the Lorenz cipher — is widely credited as the world’s first programmable electronic computer. The modern policy era began with the National Cyber Security Strategy 2016–2021 and the same-year creation of the NCSC, and the sector is now genuinely large: the government’s May 2026 Cyber Security Sectoral Analysis put it at £14.7bn in revenue and roughly 69,600 jobs across 2,600+ firms, both up sharply year-on-year. It also sits inside the most active legislative moment for UK cyber law in a decade — the Cyber Security and Resilience Bill is midway through the House of Lords, and a separate National Security Bill will bring the first reform of the 35-year-old Computer Misuse Act — layered on top of the same July 2026 machinery-of-government reshuffle affecting every sector in this tool, which leaves cyber security’s own departmental home genuinely unconfirmed as the Evolving tab sets out.', 'Links prioritise primary sources (gov.uk, parliament.uk, legislation.gov.uk, NCSC/GCHQ/NPSA/MI5, company and institution sites) over media coverage, researched August 2026. Items marked no link verified are confirmed but no URL was retrieved during research. Cybersecurity uses its own five-group Regulation taxonomy (Critical Infrastructure &amp; Essential Services / Cybercrime Law / Consumer, Product &amp; Telecoms Security / Codes of Practice &amp; Standards / Export Controls) rather than reusing Semiconductors’ four sub-tabs — a deliberate choice, since this tool does not enforce cross-sector taxonomy consistency for Regulation. The National Cyber Advisory Board (whole-economy, Cabinet Office) and the Government Cyber Advisory Board (government’s own estate, DSIT) are two genuinely distinct bodies, not a renaming of one into the other — both appear separately under Institutions. The Evolving tab collects developments that are still genuinely unfolding — most notably where cyber security policy lands within government after DSIT’s July 2026 abolition, and whether the Cyber Security and Resilience Bill’s final scope and the Computer Misuse Act’s statutory defence survive drafting — as distinct from the settled historical record in the other tabs. Notable companies (Darktrace, Sophos, NCC Group, BAE Systems Digital Intelligence) are included as anchor players within Institutions rather than exhaustively profiled — see the person’s companion research document for fuller company detail. Post-quantum cryptography migration is deliberately not duplicated here; it is covered in the Quantum sector’s Breakthroughs/Timeline. In the Breakthroughs section the subtitle field lists key people; the caveat field lists clarifying or sourcing notes. Import replaces all records for the section(s) present in the uploaded file; other sections are unaffected. Template CSV includes one guide row and one example per type — delete both before importing real data. Valid section values: timeline | institution | initiative | regulation | breakthrough | evolving. Initiative rows use type domestic or international; regulation rows use type crit-infra, cybercrime, consumer-telecoms, codes-standards or export.', '2026-08-01') ON CONFLICT (sector_id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO sectors (sector_id, label, status, framing_text, methodology_notes, last_reviewed_date) VALUES ('connectivity', 'Advanced Connectivity', 'active', 'Advanced connectivity is the sector where UK policy has been most directly shaped by a single geopolitical decision: the 2020 removal of Huawei from UK 5G networks, following US sanctions, cost the rollout an estimated year and up to £2bn, and reshaped a decade of subsequent policy — the £250m 5G Supply Chain Diversification Strategy, the UK–Japan Open RAN partnership, and SONIC Labs all trace directly back to it. Beyond vendor security, the sector spans two parallel infrastructure stories: a terrestrial build-out (Project Gigabit, the Shared Rural Network, a 2030 standalone-5G ambition) that is running behind its original targets, and a fast-moving non-terrestrial one, where the UK’s 2020 sovereign-capability bet on OneWeb has since become a minority stake in a French-led satellite group, even as Ofcom’s 2025–2026 direct-to-device satellite framework made the UK the first country in Europe to let ordinary smartphones connect straight to satellites. As in every sector in this tool, the July 2026 abolition of DSIT changed departmental ownership — telecoms returned to an expanded DCMS rather than following science and AI policy elsewhere — and, as the Evolving tab sets out, exactly who now sponsors the UK’s satellite shareholdings is still unconfirmed.', 'Scope follows the UK Government’s own definition — 5G/6G, non-terrestrial networks and advanced optical networks — deliberately excluding broader UK space policy (launch, Earth observation, space domain awareness); only the communications-relevant slice of satellite activity is covered. Links prioritise primary sources (gov.uk, legislation.gov.uk, ofcom.org.uk, company and institution sites) over media coverage, researched August 2026. Items marked no link verified are confirmed but no URL was retrieved during research. Advanced Connectivity uses its own three-group Regulation taxonomy (Spectrum Management &amp; Licensing / Infrastructure Deployment &amp; Access Rights / Satellite &amp; Non-Terrestrial Spectrum), plus the Cybersecurity sector’s existing Consumer, Product &amp; Telecoms Security key reused deliberately for two placeholder entries: the Telecommunications (Security) Act 2021 and the Huawei Designated Vendor Direction have their full, primary-sourced detail filed under Cybersecurity rather than duplicated here. Telecoms/connectivity policy has changed department five times since 2016 (DCMS → DSIT, Feb 2023 → DCMS, Jul 2026); historical entries retain the department that owned the issue at the time, while Ofcom, BDUK, UKTIN, SONIC Labs and the Wireless Infrastructure Strategy all currently sit with DCMS. The Direct-to-Device satellite framework (Regulation tab) moved from an open consultation to a settled authorisation regime with live implementation (VodafoneThree already approved) over the course of this research — flagged explicitly since an earlier draft had it as unresolved. The Evolving tab collects developments that are still genuinely unfolding — most notably which department now sponsors the UK Space Agency and the UK’s Eutelsat OneWeb shareholding post-DSIT, and the very recent (31 Jul 2026) Vodafone buyout of the remainder of VodafoneThree — as distinct from the settled historical record in the other tabs. Notable companies (BT/EE, VodafoneThree, VMO2, CityFibre, Eutelsat OneWeb, NEC, Goonhilly) are referenced within Institutions/International Initiatives where directly relevant but are not exhaustively profiled here — see the person’s companion research document for fuller company detail. The Global Coalition on Telecommunications appears here in full and, less centrally, in Semiconductors’ International Initiatives — kept in both rather than resolved. In the Breakthroughs section the subtitle field lists key people; the caveat field lists clarifying or sourcing notes. Import replaces all records for the section(s) present in the uploaded file; other sections are unaffected. Template CSV includes one guide row and one example per type — delete both before importing real data. Valid section values: timeline | institution | initiative | regulation | breakthrough | evolving. Initiative rows use type domestic or international; regulation rows use type spectrum, infra-access, satellite or consumer-telecoms.', '2026-08-01') ON CONFLICT (sector_id) DO NOTHING;
